# EXPR(1)

## NAME

expr - evaluate arguments as an expression

## SYNOPSIS

| **expr** arg **...**

## DESCRIPTION

The arguments are taken as an expression. After evaluation, the result is written on the
standard output. Each token of the expression is a separate argument.

The operators and keywords are listed below. The list is in order of increasing precedence,
with equal precedence operators grouped.

*expr | expr*
: yields the first *expr* if it is neither null nor `0`, otherwise yields the second *expr.*

*expr & expr*
: yields the first *expr* if neither *expr* is null or `0`, otherwise yields `0`.

*expr relop expr*
: where *relop is one of* < <= = != >= >, yields `1` if the indicated comparison is true, `0` if
  false. The comparison is numeric if both *expr* are integers, otherwise lexicographic.

  An integer is an optional `-` followed by one or more digits. The null string is not one.

  **Note:** v7 asked this question twice and differently — a leading `-` was allowed on the
  left operand of a comparison and on neither operand of an arithmetic operator — so
  `expr -2 < -1` compared as strings and answered `0`. A minus sign makes a number
  everywhere here.

  **Note:** v7's `<=` computed the same expression as `>=`, so it answered `0` for every
  *expr* that really was less than the other. It is a comparison here.

  A lexicographic comparison is by unsigned byte, so text in UTF-8 sorts in code point order.

*expr* + *expr*
: | *expr* - *expr*
  | addition or subtraction of the arguments.

*expr* \* *expr*
: | *expr* / *expr*
  | *expr* % *expr*
  | multiplication, division, or remainder of the arguments.

*expr* : *expr*
: The matching operator compares the string first argument with the regular expression second
  argument; regular expression syntax is that of ed(1) with the addition of the interval
  \\{*n*,*m*\\}. The match is anchored at the beginning of the first argument. The
  **\\(...\\)** pattern symbols can be used to select a portion of the first argument, and at
  most one such group is allowed; a group that did not match yields the null string.
  Otherwise, the matching operator yields the number of characters matched (`0` on failure).

( *expr* )
: parentheses for grouping. A parenthesis is an operator only when it is the whole argument,
  so `expr (3)` yields the string `(3)`.

  **Note:** v7 looked at the first character alone, and made that a syntax error.

Examples:

To add 1 to the Shell variable *a*:

> a=\`expr $a + 1\`

To find the filename part (least significant part) of the pathname stored in variable *a,*
which may or may not contain `/`:

> expr $a : '.\*/\\(.\*\\)' '|' $a

Note the quoted Shell metacharacters.

## ARITHMETIC IS ONE MACHINE WORD

An integer here is one 41-bit word, so the range is -1099511627776 through 1099511627775.
Overflow is not detected and wraps silently.

Division or remainder by zero is a diagnostic and exit code `2`. v7 left it to the machine,
which on this one raises a division-by-zero exception rather than answering wrongly.

## EVERYTHING COUNTS BYTES

*length,* *substr,* *index* and the count the matching operator yields are all in bytes, not
characters, so `expr length привет` is `12`. A character class holds any byte value, so a
class may contain the bytes of a multi-byte letter and a range between two of them is a range
of bytes.

*substr* yields the null string when the starting position is less than 1 or past the end of
the string, or when the length is not positive. v7 did not return from a starting position of
zero at all.

## SEE ALSO

ed(1), sh(1), test(1)

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Expr* returns the following exit codes:

| 0 if the expression is neither null nor `0`,
| 1 if the expression is null or `0`,
| 2 for invalid expressions.

The diagnostics are `syntax error`, `non-numeric argument`, `division by zero`, `RE error`,
`too many \\(` and `expression too complex`, each preceded by `expr: `. The last of these is
a limit on how deeply the matcher may recurse, which is one level per `*` in the regular
expression; twelve levels are allowed, and nothing anyone writes comes near it.
